        "## Step 11 — Pollutant relationships: Spearman, Pearson and sample sizes\n",
        "\n",
        "Pooled correlations can be misleading if stations have different typical concentration levels. We therefore use **within-station centred values** for the primary relationship matrix: for each pollutant, subtract that station’s median from its daily values. The Spearman matrix then describes whether pollutants tend to move together within stations, rather than merely reflecting different station baselines. Pearson correlation is exported as a linear-association sensitivity check. Pairwise sample sizes are reported because pollutant coverage differs, especially for CO and O3."
      ]
